import{_ as t,o as r,c as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework — the driver framework","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"release/3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework.md","filePath":"release/3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework.md"}'),a={name:"release/3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework.md"};function d(s,e,n,c,l,h){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="_3-0-0-wp08-driver-framework-—-the-driver-framework" tabindex="-1">3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework — the driver framework <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-0-0-wp08-driver-framework-—-the-driver-framework" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework — the driver framework&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The L0 driver-framework batch: the six capabilities that turn &quot;add a driver by adding a file&quot; from a naming convention into an enforced engineering standard - metadata, a device tree, power hooks, hotplug events, failure isolation and a deterministic init order. The legacy <code>OC_DRIVER_DECL</code> path is untouched and coexists (existing drivers needed zero changes; both registration paths are collected at boot).</p><h2 id="changes" tabindex="-1">Changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changes&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Driver metadata and dependencies.</strong> A new descriptor - <code>oc_driver_info_t</code> (name, version, author, license, dependency list, compatible device types, priority hint, D-state mask, and init/probe/remove/suspend/resume callbacks) - registers through <code>OC_DRIVER_INFO_DECL</code> into the new <code>.oc_drvi</code> linker section. The old macro keeps its exact signature and behavior.</li><li><strong>A device tree without ACPI.</strong> Node model (id, parent, children, type, MMIO/IOPORT/IRQ/DMA resources, binding state, private data), register/unregister, lookup by id or name, by-type / by-child / depth-first iteration. The framework parses no ACPI and enumerates no bus - WP-09a/WP-09b will fill the tree through the same register calls.</li><li><strong>Driver-level power hooks.</strong> <code>oc_pm_notify(D1..D3)</code> suspends children-first, <code>oc_pm_notify(D0)</code> resumes in reverse; drivers that never declared the target state are skipped. No S-state machine, no policy - WP-22 calls the interface.</li><li><strong>A generic hotplug event queue.</strong> IRQ-safe <code>oc_hotplug_post()</code>, process-context <code>oc_hotplug_drain()</code>, filtered subscriptions by device type or node id, drop-and-count overflow accounting. The concrete bus triggers stay with WP-09a/WP-14a.</li><li><strong>Failure isolation.</strong> A probe or init failure is recorded (driver, error code, count, timestamp), the node stays visible as unbound-with-error, and the boot carries on - never a panic, never a blocked walk. <code>drv reload</code> retries; the ledger clears only on a clean comeback.</li><li><strong>A deterministic init order.</strong> Topological over the declared dependencies (Kahn&#39;s algorithm, priority-hint + name tie-break) - byte-identical across reboots and rebuilds, never link order. Cycles and missing dependencies are isolated with a report instead of a silent loop.</li><li><strong>Application dictionary.</strong> Full re-capture on this image: 308 commands (284 + the 16 new), every entry a real executed transcript in both languages (the verify gate passes 308 = registry = enumeration = EN = zh).</li></ul><h2 id="new-interfaces" tabindex="-1">New interfaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#new-interfaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;New interfaces&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>OC_DRIVER_INFO_DECL</code> (ocos/drivers/framework/interface/core_drv_meta.h) <ul><li>the metadata registration macro (.oc_drvi section)</li></ul></li><li><code>oc_devtree_register/unregister/find/find_name/add_res</code> and the by-type / by-child / DFS walkers (core_devtree.h) - the device tree</li><li><code>oc_pm_notify</code> / <code>oc_pm_nodes_in_dstate</code> (core_pm_hook.h) - the driver power-state change request (for WP-22)</li><li><code>oc_hotplug_post / drain / subscribe / unsubscribe</code> and the per-owner delivery stats (core_hotplug.h) - the event surface (for WP-09a/WP-14a)</li><li><code>oc_drv_fail_note/count/at/clear</code> (core_drv_isolation.h) - the failure query interface</li><li><code>oc_drvfw_boot/bind/unbind/reload/detach_subtree/bind_pass</code> (core_drv_registry.h) - the registry operations</li><li><code>svc_diag_drvfw_dispatch</code> (services/diag/interface/svc_diag_drvfw.h) <ul><li>the shared diag-surface implementation</li></ul></li><li><code>l1_diag_drv_cmd/dev_cmd/drv_sub/dev_sub</code> (ocos/interface/l1_diag_cmds.h) - the shared ring-3 command layer</li></ul><h2 id="new-shell-commands" tabindex="-1">New shell commands <a class="header-anchor" href="#new-shell-commands" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;New shell commands&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>drv</code> - driver framework diagnostics (umbrella; subcommands list, info, failed, bind, unbind, reload, debug, order, help)</li><li><code>dev</code> - device tree diagnostics (umbrella; subcommands tree, list, info, debug, help)</li><li><code>drv_list</code> <code>drv_info</code> <code>drv_failed</code> <code>drv_bind</code> <code>drv_unbind</code><code>drv_reload</code> <code>drv_debug</code> <code>drv_order</code> <code>drv_help</code> - the standalone twins of the drv subcommands (byte-identical output)</li><li><code>dev_tree</code> <code>dev_list</code> <code>dev_info</code> <code>dev_debug</code> <code>dev_help</code> - the standalone twins of the dev subcommands</li></ul><h2 id="assets-sha256" tabindex="-1">Assets (SHA256) <a class="header-anchor" href="#assets-sha256" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Assets (SHA256)&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Size</th><th>SHA256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>opencube-3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework.iso</td><td>4397056</td><td>d559b8acfebc6ff3cbbe81d3c1c6ccfe8a9bc812718a716400e5730f4645d1fd</td></tr><tr><td>OpenCubeOS-src-3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework.zip</td><td>7303991</td><td>c6508483392cde3e698aeb6e92eb5549a99c82472faa67e92e4ab16e005f4c10</td></tr><tr><td>OpenCubeOS-3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework-update.tar.gz</td><td>1512893</td><td>34e054e35c016ee12329f341ad918a2ee24ef948562adf9cd2f1a338e4d7c104</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The assets were re-cut on 2026-11-19 from the tagged tree <code>v3.0.0-WP08-driver-framework</code> (= the shipped tree, including the pool fixup). Rebuild proof: the ISO is <strong>bit-identical</strong> to the recorded SHA above (fresh toolchain, same gcc 14.2.0); the src zip follows the tag (the earlier cut predated the pool fixup - this one ships the exact source of the binaries); the OTA tar.gz container embeds build-time stamps (tar/gzip mtimes, manifest date) so its container bytes differ per cut while the payload - the kernel ELF, byte-identical to the ISO&#39;s - and the manifest payload digest are stable. See <code>evidence/wp08-driver-framework/11-rebuild-reproducibility.txt</code>.</p><h2 id="test-evidence" tabindex="-1">Test evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#test-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Test evidence&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Build: 0 errors; 1 pre-existing ld RWX segment notice (OCBL EFI, byte-identical with the baseline)</li><li>WP-08 QEMU acceptance gate: 43/43 (both registration paths, order <ul><li>cycle report, tree walks, d-state queries, hotplug chain, isolation, 13 byte-identical umbrella/twin pairs, error paths, zero-global-table proof, three-boot order determinism)</li></ul></li><li>WP-08 host unit test: 54/54 (the framework compiled and driven with no ACPI, no bus, no QEMU; PM hook ordering asserted callback-by-callback)</li><li>Regression: 31/31; SSH E2E: 5/5; HTTPS E2E: 8/8; dhtest: full PASS (group 1 identities, group 14 fixed vectors, 5/5 sweep)</li><li>BIOS + UEFI boots: banner + ring-3 ush&gt; + boot complete</li><li>Dictionary gate: 308 = registry = enumeration = EN = zh, every entry with a real transcript</li><li>Re-cut verification (2026-11-19): every gate above re-run PASS on the re-cut artifacts (43/43, 31/31, SSH 5/5, HTTPS 8/8, dhtest, BIOS + UEFI banners build 477, dictionary 308) - so the published set is covered end-to-end by the final (pool-fixed) build, not only the pre-fixup one</li></ul><h2 id="known-issues" tabindex="-1">Known issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known issues&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>K-04 - <code>pmmrace</code> can panic the kernel (pre-existing).</strong> The PMM race detector&#39;s own race (non-atomic bitmap vs the IRQ-context allocator) can corrupt live frames; reproduced on the previous release baseline (f5af2ab). This batch&#39;s boot work shifts the IRQ/loop phase and makes the fatal interleaving likelier. The fix is PMM IRQ-atomicity - a separate work package. Until then a <code>pmmrace</code> PASS verdict only covers the rounds it survived.</li><li><strong>K-05 - a fresh clone can rotate the OCIRE key.</strong> <code>ocire.mk</code> regenerates the Ed25519 public key whenever the generator script&#39;s mtime beats the committed file&#39;s; the rotated key is committed with this release so git matches the artifacts. Consequence (2026-11-19): the private half of this keypair lived only on the original build host and is lost with it - signature-verified OCIRE update payloads for this anchor are not producible (no release asset depends on them; the OTA flow uses the sha256 manifest). The next <code>gen</code> rotation re-anchors the keypair.</li></ul><p>Evidence: <code>evidence/wp08-driver-framework/</code> (boot logs, gate and test outputs, the K-04 reproduction transcripts).</p>',16)])])}const m=t(a,[["render",d]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
